LESSONS_I8 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Advanced inventory: scope and structure", "11", "<p>This module completes the Inventory track with the structural layer: the settings, the register of control decisions, the item lifecycle, the role design, the records, and the multi-company structure. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the Stock Settings walk and operate its annual review; maintain the stock control register; run the item lifecycle from the creation gate to the retirement flow; operate the role matrix with the recertification and the adjustments read; apply the records and handover conventions; and configure multi-company stock structure correctly.</p><p><b>Why the structural layer is its own module.</b> Modules 1-7 taught the operating procedures. Each of those procedures depends on decisions made once and held: which settings are on, who holds which powers, how items enter and leave the register, where the evidence files. When the structural layer is maintained, the procedures run the same way regardless of who is present; when it is not, the procedures degrade into individual habits. The module's standing test — Chapter 8's worked example — is whether the stock function operates correctly through an extended absence of its most experienced people.</p><p><b>The three instruments.</b></p><p>1. <b>The Stock Settings page</b> (Chapter 2) — the system-wide switches, each set by decision and reviewed annually.<br>2. <b>The control register</b> (Chapter 3) — the one-table record of every stock control decision: what is set, to what value, decided by whom, for what reason, reviewed when.<br>3. <b>The lifecycle and access procedures</b> (Chapters 4-5) — how items and permissions change: by gate, by governance, by recertification.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The stock manager maintains the register and runs the reads; the owner decides the settings and the powers at the annual review and at every change; the sysadmin executes permission changes per the Sysadmin track's procedures. Setting changes follow the standing change discipline: written first, decided by the named owner, applied in a maintenance window where the change affects posting behaviour, and recorded in the register with the date of application.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Stock Settings walk. Chapter 3: the control register. Chapter 4: the item lifecycle. Chapter 5: roles, access, and the adjustments read. Chapter 6: records and handover. Chapter 7: multi-company structure. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review, the module rule, and the track recap.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Stock Settings walk", "13", "<p>Open Stock Settings (awesome bar). Walk the fields in order. Each carries a recommended posture, and every departure from it is a register entry with a reason.</p><p><b>1. Item defaults.</b></p><p>1. <b>Item Naming By</b> — item code entry convention (manual codes per the written coding scheme, or a naming series). Decided once at implementation; changing it mid-register is a governed event.<br>2. <b>Default Warehouse</b> — the final fallback of module 1's resolution chain. Set to the main central store; the monthly fallback read (module 1, Chapter 5) treats anything landing here as a wiring gap.<br>3. <b>Default Valuation Method</b> — the method items inherit where their own field is unset (module 5, Chapter 3). Set per the accountant's policy; the item-level field carries the exceptions.</p><p><b>2. Stock transaction controls.</b></p><p>1. <b>Allow Negative Stock</b> — <b>off.</b> The recommended posture from modules 1 and 5: a posting that would drive a balance negative is refused, forcing the missing document to be entered instead. The register records the posture and the module 5 remediation that enforced it.<br>2. <b>Over-delivery and over-receipt allowances</b> — the percentage tolerances for deliveries and receipts exceeding their orders. Set low per the policy; the tolerance is a control decision because it defines how far a document can depart from its authorisation before the system refuses.<br>3. <b>Role Allowed to Over-deliver/Receive</b> — where exceptional tolerance is granted, the named role holds it, per the register.</p><p><b>3. The freeze controls.</b></p><p>1. <b>Stock Frozen Up To</b> (date) — postings dated on or before this date are blocked for ordinary roles. Advanced with each period close per the Accounts track's calendar, and set for count windows (module 4, Chapter 3).<br>2. <b>Stock Frozen Up To (days)</b> — the rolling variant: postings older than the set number of days are blocked, which enforces module 2's same-day discipline as a system rule rather than a briefing point.<br>3. <b>Role Allowed to Edit Frozen Stock</b> — the exception role for postings into frozen dates. Held narrowly per the matrix; every use carries its reason per module 2's backdating lane.</p><p><b>4. Batch, serial, and reorder controls.</b> The system-level defaults that support module 3's identity settings and module 6's reorder machinery — including the automatic Material Request generation switch for reorder rows. Each is set to serve the written procedures, and the reorder automation in particular is enabled only after module 6's levels are set from data, because automation ordering on unset levels generates requests nobody reviewed.</p><p><b>5. The annual settings review.</b> Once a year, the stock manager and the owner walk this page against the control register: each setting confirmed still correct, each departure re-justified or corrected, the review dated in the register. Settings drift when nobody reads them; the annual walk is the standing correction, and the review's output is the register's next revision.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The stock control register", "12", "<p>The control register is one table holding every stock control decision. This chapter is its structure and operation.</p><p><b>1. The row format.</b> Per control: the control's name, its current value or posture, the owner who decided it, the reason in one line, the date decided, and the next review date. One row per control; the register lives with the stock function's documents and is version-kept — superseded rows retained with their dates, so any past posture is answerable.</p><p><b>2. The register's contents.</b> The rows collect the decisions modules 1-7 made, in one place:</p><p>1. <b>Structure:</b> the warehouse tree convention and level rule (module 1); the special warehouses in use; the default wiring convention.<br>2. <b>Movement:</b> the named Stock Entry Types; the issue and receipt lawful-case tables with the approval ladder; the backdating posture and its role; the transfer hygiene rules.<br>3. <b>Identity:</b> the item-class control table — batch posture, expiry, serials per class (module 3, Chapter 2); the recall procedure's owner and drill calendar.<br>4. <b>Counting:</b> the count cadences per warehouse; the threshold ladder's bands; the ABC review dates; the accuracy target.<br>5. <b>Valuation:</b> the method policy per class; the landed-cost consignment rule; the tie-out procedure; the revaluation lane's owner.<br>6. <b>Network:</b> the replenishment cadence; the allocation rules with the essential floor list; the branch purchasing boundary and its exception threshold; the lateral approval rule.<br>7. <b>Health:</b> the aging bands and the over-ninety target; the markdown ladder; the probation window; the review calendar.<br>8. <b>Settings:</b> every Chapter 2 posture with its reason.</p><p><b>3. Why one table.</b> Distributed decisions are unauditable: the answer to how the stock function is controlled should not require reading seven procedures. The register is the one-lookup answer — for the owner's annual review, the external auditor's walkthrough, the new stock manager's first week, and the absence test (Chapter 8). It also exposes contradictions: two procedures carrying different thresholds for the same decision are visible only when both sit in one table.</p><p><b>4. The register's maintenance.</b> Every control change lands in the register the day it is decided — the change is not complete until its row is updated. The quarterly review confirms the register against the live system (the settings page, the role matrix, the procedure documents); differences are corrected in whichever direction the owner's decision requires. A register that trails the system is a record of what used to be true, which for control purposes is the same as no register.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The item lifecycle", "12", "<p>Items enter the register by gate, change by governance, and leave by the retirement flow. This chapter is the three procedures.</p><p><b>1. The creation gate.</b> New items are created against the checklist, by the roles the matrix permits:</p><p>1. <b>The duplicate check first</b> — the register searched by name fragments, barcode, and supplier reference before creation. A duplicate item splits one product's history across two records: stock in two places, reorder arithmetic on half the data, and counts that disagree by construction. The gate's search takes one minute; the merge remediation later takes days.<br>2. <b>The class row applied</b> — module 3's control table: the identity flags per the item's class, set at creation because retro-fitting over existing stock is a remediation.<br>3. <b>The defaults wired</b> — module 1's Item Defaults row per the convention; the UoM and conversion table per module 2's register where the item converts.<br>4. <b>The commercial fields per their tracks</b> — item group, prices, tax template (the Selling and Accounts tracks' territory), completed per the checklist so the item is fully operable at first receipt.<br>5. <b>The probation flag</b> — module 7's new-item window starts at creation.</p><p><b>2. Change governance.</b> Item changes that affect posting behaviour — the valuation method, the identity flags, the UoM structure, the item group where accounts derive from it — are governed events per the standing change discipline: decided by the named owner, dated, and recorded. Descriptive changes (names, descriptions, images) are ordinary edits. The distinction is written in the procedure so the difference is a rule, not a judgment call per edit.</p><p><b>3. The retirement flow.</b> Items leave the register by procedure, never by deletion:</p><p>1. <b>The delisting decision</b> — from the range review (module 7, Chapter 6), recorded with its reason.<br>2. <b>Stock resolved through the lanes</b> — the remaining balance cleared per module 7's preference order; the flow does not complete while stock remains.<br>3. <b>The machinery disconnected</b> — reorder rows removed at every site; the item taken off active price lists and offers per the selling procedures.<br>4. <b>Disabled at zero</b> — the item's Disabled flag set once every warehouse's balance is zero. The record remains readable forever: ledger history, past documents, and reports all reference it, which is why deletion is never the instrument.<br>5. <b>The execution status tracked</b> — module 7's rule: each delisting on the review's list until all four steps close.</p><p><b>4. The lifecycle reads.</b> Monthly: items created in the month against the checklist (sampled); the duplicate-candidate report where the site runs one (near-identical names flagged for review). Quarterly: disabled items with non-zero balances (a retirement flow that skipped its steps — corrected through the lanes); active items with no movement and no probation flag feeding module 7's classification.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Roles, access, and the adjustments read", "12", "<p>The stock role design was built across modules 1-7. This chapter consolidates it and adds the standing reads that keep it true.</p><p><b>1. The matrix, consolidated.</b> The stock section of the site's permission matrix, one row per power:</p><p>1. <b>Stock User</b> — daily movement documents within site scope (module 1's user permissions).<br>2. <b>Stock Manager</b> — reconciliations, backdated postings, cancellations and amendments, the settings pages, lateral and allocation approvals. Held by named individuals, not by convenience.<br>3. <b>The exception roles</b> — frozen-stock editing, over-delivery tolerance, the recall freeze authority: each a register row with its named holders.<br>4. <b>The separations</b> — the keeper does not approve their own adjustments (module 1); the counter does not enter their own counts (module 4); the preparer and reviewer split on value-moving documents (module 2).</p><p><b>2. The quarterly recertification.</b> Per the Sysadmin track's procedure, applied to stock: the matrix's stock rows walked against the live role assignments; leavers and movers confirmed removed (the HR lifecycle feed); exception-role holders re-confirmed by the owner; the recertification dated in the register.</p><p><b>3. The adjustments read.</b> Monthly, network-wide: every Stock Reconciliation and every Material Issue above the de-minimis band, listed with its author, approver, warehouse, and value. The read answers two questions:</p><p>1. <b>Concentration</b> — adjustment powers should resolve to a small set of named people. The healthy result is that the month's adjustments across the network trace to the named Stock Manager holders and no one else; an unfamiliar name on the list is a permission drift found by reading rather than by incident.<br>2. <b>Pattern</b> — the same warehouse, item class, or author recurring across months joins module 4's pattern file. The read is data assembly; conclusions and responses belong to the owner per the standing rule.</p><p><b>4. The access boundary cases, stated.</b> Temporary elevation (the deputy covering leave) is granted by the procedure with an expiry date and removed on return — the recertification checks expired grants specifically. Shared logins are prohibited for stock roles without exception: every adjustment must resolve to an individual, because the separations of point 1 are meaningless between accounts two people share. Service and integration accounts that post stock documents are register rows like human holders, with their scopes stated.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Records and handover", "12", "<p>The track's procedures generate evidence. This chapter is where it files, how long it is kept, and how a role hands over.</p><p><b>1. The filing map.</b> One location per file class, named in the procedure:</p><p>1. <b>Count files</b> — per count: signed sheets, the variance report, investigation notes, the posted reconciliation reference (module 4). Filed per warehouse per date.<br>2. <b>Recall files</b> — per event or drill: the notice, timings, snapshot, transfers, trace, disposition (module 3, Chapter 7).<br>3. <b>Remediation files</b> — per project: scope, before-state, batches, verification (module 5's discipline).<br>4. <b>Review packs</b> — monthly: the health page with its decision list and worksheets (modules 6-7); the run notes and allocation worksheets (module 6).<br>5. <b>The register and matrix</b> — current versions plus superseded rows (Chapters 3, 5).</p><p><b>2. Retention.</b> Stock records support financial reporting and, for regulated goods, compliance obligations: retention follows the longer of the accounting retention the Accounts track sets and the regulatory retention the compliance owner sets, per record class, written in the filing map. Nothing in the map is deleted informally; disposal at end of retention is itself a logged action per the site's records procedure.</p><p><b>3. The one-lookup standard, restated as a test.</b> Any question about any stock event — a count adjustment two years back, a recall's customer list, an allocation's arithmetic — is answerable from one file location in one sitting. The quarterly test: the stock manager picks three past events and retrieves each file; a retrieval that requires reconstruction from memory or from multiple locations is a filing-map correction.</p><p><b>4. The handover file.</b> Per role, maintained current rather than written at departure:</p><p>1. <b>The stock manager's file</b> — the register, the matrix section, the review calendar, the run procedure (module 6's closing notes), the open items list (current investigations, in-flight remediations, pending delistings).<br>2. <b>The keeper's file per warehouse</b> — the custody row, the count cadence, the site's procedures, the current exceptions.<br>3. <b>The standard:</b> a competent successor operates the role from the file within a week, with questions but without reconstruction. Chapter 8's absence test is this standard, tested by an absence rather than a departure.</p><p><b>5. The records read.</b> Riding the quarterly recertification: the filing map walked, the retrieval test run, the handover files confirmed current (the open-items lists dated within the quarter). Records maintenance is scheduled work; records reconstructed at need are the expensive alternative the schedule exists to prevent.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Multi-company structure", "12", "<p>Where the site runs more than one legal entity, stock structure follows the legal boundary. This chapter is the rules.</p><p><b>1. Per-company structure.</b> Each company holds its own warehouse tree from its own root (module 1, Chapter 3), its own default wiring, and its own settings postures where the system scopes them per company. The control register carries per-company rows where postures differ, and the annual settings review walks each company.</p><p><b>2. The item register decision.</b> Items are shared across companies on one register, with per-company rows carrying the defaults (module 1's Item Defaults per company) and the accounts derivations per the Accounts track. The decision recorded in the register: which item classes both companies trade, and which are single-company by policy — because a shared register with unshared intentions produces items ordered by the wrong entity through an unconsidered default.</p><p><b>3. Inter-company stock movement.</b> Stock crossing the legal boundary is a sale and a purchase, not a transfer:</p><p>1. <b>The documents</b> — the selling company's Delivery Note and Sales Invoice; the buying company's Purchase Receipt and Purchase Invoice; the inter-company document links where the site's configuration creates the counterpart automatically. The Accounts track owns the pricing and settlement rules; this module's rule is the stock side: <b>an internal Material Transfer never crosses a company boundary.</b><br>2. <b>Why</b> — a cross-company transfer moves value between legal entities with no sale recorded: both companies' accounts misstate, and the correction is an accounting remediation. The system's company scoping refuses the direct case; the rule exists for the indirect ones (the shared building's informal handover between two entities' shelves).<br>3. <b>The shared-building procedure</b> — where two entities occupy one site (module 1, Chapter 3), the physical segregation is explicit, the keepers know which shelves belong to which company's warehouses, and any goods crossing between them move by the inter-company documents on the same day the goods move.</p><p><b>4. Identity and counts across companies.</b> Batch and serial records follow the item register's sharing: a batch received by one company and sold to the other carries through both companies' documents, and a recall trace (module 3) runs across both entities' delivery records — the recall file notes the entity per holding. Counts run per company per warehouse; the accuracy metric reports per company; and the tie-out (module 5) runs per company's stock accounts, because each entity's balance sheet stands alone.</p><p><b>5. The multi-company read.</b> Quarterly: inter-company stock document pairs reconciled (every despatch matched to its counterpart receipt and both invoices); unmatched pairs investigated as the month-end items they are. The read belongs to the accounts close checklist; the stock function's part is the document discipline that keeps the read short.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: the structural year and the absence test", "13", "<p>This chapter runs Chapters 2-7 through HealthTrade's structural year. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the settings walk with the MD (Chapter 2).</b> The first annual review walks Stock Settings against the draft register: the negative-stock posture confirmed off (module 5's remediation recorded as its reason); the freeze controls aligned to the close calendar with the rolling-days rule enforcing same-day posting; the over-delivery tolerance reduced to the policy figure with the exception role named; reorder automation confirmed on only for the sites whose levels were set from data. The walk's output is the register's first complete revision — every posture with an owner, a reason, and a review date.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the adjustments read establishes its baseline (Chapter 5).</b> The first monthly read lists the network's reconciliations and banded issues: every adjustment traces to <b>two names network-wide</b> — the named Stock Manager holders — and no one else. The result files as the baseline; the read thereafter is a five-minute confirmation that the concentration holds, and one quarter's list showing a third name resolves to a documented temporary elevation with its expiry date, verified against the register.</p><p><b>Step 3 — the creation gate catches its case (Chapter 4).</b> A new-item request duplicates an existing line under a supplier's alternative product name; the gate's duplicate search matches on the barcode and stops the creation. The counterfactual files in the training note: the duplicate would have split the item's history, doubled its reorder arithmetic, and produced count disagreements at every site stocking both records.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the retirement flow at scale (Chapter 4).</b> The range review's delisting batch — <b>60 delistings</b> — executes through the four-step flow: stock resolved through module 7's lanes, reorder rows removed at every site, price lists cleared, each item disabled at zero. Execution status tracks on the review list until all sixty close; two items found with residual balances mid-flow are returned to the lanes rather than disabled over stock.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the absence test (Chapters 3-6).</b> The founder — the business's most experienced stock decision-maker — is abroad for <b>three weeks</b>. The structural layer's test:</p><p>1. The weekly replenishment runs execute from the run procedure and closing notes; the deputy approves per the documented ladder.<br>2. The <b>two-name controls hold for the twenty-one days</b> — the temporary elevation granted by procedure with its expiry, and removed on return, verified at the next recertification.<br>3. <b>One incident — a transit shortfall</b> — is resolved entirely from the documents: the despatch entry, the receiving count, the investigation lane, the closure under the ladder, per module 2's procedure. The resolution requires no individual's recollection: the file answers every question the investigation asks.<br>4. The monthly health page compiles on schedule by the deputy from the documented method.</p><p>The test's conclusion files with the register review: the stock function operates from its structure. The findings list is short — one procedure step ambiguously worded, corrected; one handover file's open-items list found a month stale, corrected and added to the quarterly records read.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Review, the module rule, and the track recap", "12", "<p>The errors that recur in the structural layer, the module rule, and the track's eight rules.</p><p><b>Error 1 — settings nobody owns.</b> Symptom: postures set at implementation and never reviewed; departures nobody can explain. Correction: the settings walk run, the register built. Prevention: the annual review with the owner, dated in the register.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the register that trails the system.</b> Symptom: control changes made but not recorded; the register describing a past configuration. Correction: the quarterly confirmation against the live system. Prevention: the rule that a change is not complete until its row is updated.</p><p><b>Error 3 — items created around the gate.</b> Symptom: duplicates splitting histories; identity flags missing at first receipt; defaults absent. Correction: duplicates merged by remediation; flags and defaults completed. Prevention: the creation gate as the only entry path, with the monthly sample read.</p><p><b>Error 4 — deletion as retirement.</b> Symptom: items deleted with history referencing them; reports and documents breaking. Correction: records restored per the incident lane. Prevention: the retirement flow — lanes, disconnection, disabled at zero, never deleted.</p><p><b>Error 5 — adjustment powers spreading.</b> Symptom: reconciliations posted by names outside the matrix; separations collapsing quietly. Correction: the permissions corrected; the affected adjustments reviewed. Prevention: the monthly adjustments read against the named holders, and the quarterly recertification.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the transfer across the company boundary.</b> Symptom: stock moved between entities without sale documents; both balance sheets misstating. Correction: the accounting remediation with the inter-company documents entered. Prevention: the rule that an internal transfer never crosses a company boundary, and the quarterly pair reconciliation.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the Stock Settings walk with the recommended postures and the annual review; maintain the control register with its row format and quarterly confirmation; run the item lifecycle — the creation gate, change governance, the four-step retirement flow, and the lifecycle reads; operate the consolidated matrix, the recertification, and the adjustments read with its concentration and pattern questions; apply the filing map, retention rules, the retrieval test, and the handover files; and configure multi-company structure with the inter-company document rule.</p><p><b>Module rule 8: the stock function runs on structure — settings decided, registered, and reviewed annually; items entering by gate and leaving by the retirement flow; powers named, separated, recertified, and read monthly; evidence filed to the one-lookup standard; and the whole operable through any absence from its documents alone.</b></p><p><b>The track's eight rules.</b></p><p>1. A warehouse is a named accountability unit — designed into a shallow tree by written convention, stocked only at leaf level, wired into defaults once and verified, held by a named keeper, and changed only by document.<br>2. Every internal movement is a Stock Entry with its purpose — transfers received against what physically arrived, issues and receipts carrying reason, account, evidence, and approval, repacks held to their register, and every entry posted on its honest date.<br>3. Identity travels with the stock — captured once from the physical goods at the dock, named on every movement and confirmed at every handover, expiries read on their horizons, and any batch freezable and locatable across the network in minutes.<br>4. A count proves custody — blind sheets counted and entered by separate hands, variances recounted then investigated before anything posts, adjustments approved by band with their evidence attached, and accuracy measured on a cycle that costs minutes a day.<br>5. Value follows the documents — rates born at their four doors and nowhere else, landed costs on every named consignment, methods set by recorded policy, the stock ledger tied to the accounts at zero every month, backdating carrying its repost cost consciously, and every strange rate stopped at entry.<br>6. The network replenishes by procedure — requests reasoned on projected quantities, a weekly run with reasoned adjustments, scarcity allocated by written rule and shown as arithmetic, branches opened by transfer and reviewed weekly for ninety days, and every flow through the standard documents with a recorded reason.<br>7. Stock health is read on schedule and resolved by lane — aging and movement compiled monthly by the documented method, every listed item assigned a lane with its document, rebalancing before markdown and write-off last, findings routed upstream, and the whole reported on one page whose first figure is the accuracy of the records it stands on.<br>8. The stock function runs on structure — settings registered and reviewed, items by gate and retirement flow, powers named and read, evidence to the one-lookup standard, and the whole operable through any absence from its documents alone.</p>"),
]

